WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.057 --> 00:00:05.900
"Beauty is only skin deep."

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.642
So says a well-known proverb in English.

3
00:00:09.382 --> 00:00:17.888
It expresses the sentiment that personality, intellect, and spirit—the qualities beneath the skin—are as important as, or more

4
00:00:17.948 --> 00:00:21.891
important than, physical appearance when judging a person's worth.

5
00:00:22.611 --> 00:00:28.175
But what about people who go to great lengths to change their bodies, treating their skin like an

6
00:00:28.315 --> 00:00:29.176
artist's canvas?

7
00:00:29.844 --> 00:00:33.067
Would they agree that appearance is less important than character?

8
00:00:33.827 --> 00:00:37.951
Many who tattoo, pierce, and scar their bodies see no contradiction.

9
00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:43.976
For them, body art, as these practices are known, is an expression of inner beauty.

10
00:00:44.996 --> 00:00:50.721
The practice of decorating the body, with ink, metal, or wood, goes back to the days of ancient

11
00:00:50.821 --> 00:00:54.124
Egypt and perhaps earlier, to Mesopotamian times.

12
00:00:54.768 --> 00:01:00.390
The form of body art most widespread through history is the simple painting or coloring of the skin.

13
00:01:01.211 --> 00:01:07.934
Ancient Egyptians used kohl, a powder made from soot and other ingredients, to darken and decorate the eyes.

14
00:01:08.734 --> 00:01:14.717
Some groups of Native Americans painted their bodies and faces with colored clay before going into battle.

15
00:01:15.337 --> 00:01:21.860
Ancient cultures in northern Africa and India stained the skin in intricate patterns by grinding the leaves of

16
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.425
the henna plant and applying the paste with a fine brush.

17
00:01:26.546 --> 00:01:33.549
These forms of decoration may have had practical origins; kohl protected the eyes from the sun's glare, and

18
00:01:33.649 --> 00:01:37.290
face paint helped warriors identify fellow members of their tribe.

19
00:01:37.971 --> 00:01:41.853
But the decorations were also intended to change the wearer's appearance.

20
00:01:42.533 --> 00:01:49.276
Kohl enhanced the shape of a woman's eyes, making her more attractive—just as mascara and eyeliner do today.

21
00:01:50.036 --> 00:01:55.520
War paint made a warrior's face look grotesque or horrific in order to intimidate the enemy.

22
00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.102
Frequently, ancient cosmetics also played a ceremonial role.

23
00:02:00.883 --> 00:02:08.988
Egyptian priests and pharaohs wore kohl when performing religious rites, Native Americans painted their faces for tribal ceremonies,

24
00:02:09.628 --> 00:02:15.212
and women in almost every culture where the plant was known applied henna tattoos for their weddings.

25
00:02:16.348 --> 00:02:24.032
Most people nowadays do not consider tattoos, scars, or piercings to have any religious significance, but cultures the

26
00:02:24.093 --> 00:02:27.915
world over have decorated their bodies to reflect spiritual beliefs.

27
00:02:28.695 --> 00:02:35.579
In sacred ceremonies, Australian Aborigines made shallow cuts on the torsos of both boys and girls when they

28
00:02:35.639 --> 00:02:36.520
reached adolescence.

29
00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:42.663
When the cuts healed, the scars showed that the child had gone through the rites necessary to become

30
00:02:42.703 --> 00:02:44.144
an active member of the tribe.

31
00:02:44.796 --> 00:02:49.219
The scars also indicated the boy or girl's relationship with the spirit world.

32
00:02:49.859 --> 00:02:57.604
Scarification, as this practice is called, was once widespread in Australia, but as a ritual practice, it is

33
00:02:57.644 --> 00:03:00.686
now restricted mainly to a remote northeastern territory.

34
00:03:01.866 --> 00:03:07.069
In the Sepik region of Papua New Guinea, the skin of adolescent boys is sliced with a bamboo

35
00:03:07.109 --> 00:03:11.312
sliver to test the boys' self-discipline and initiate them into manhood.

36
00:03:12.048 --> 00:03:15.771
These people believe that the human race was created by crocodiles.

37
00:03:16.312 --> 00:03:22.377
The slicing creates a pattern of scars like the teeth marks of a crocodile, symbolizing that the boy

38
00:03:22.617 --> 00:03:26.481
was swallowed by the ancestral crocodile and reborn as a man.

39
00:03:27.221 --> 00:03:34.127
In other cultures, body art had no spiritual dimension, but was merely a way to display social status.

40
00:03:34.688 --> 00:03:39.212
Priests of the ancient Aztec empire wore jewelry in piercings in their lower lips.

41
00:03:39.888 --> 00:03:46.093
The jewelry was made of silver, gold, and precious stones and was considered a sign of power; the

42
00:03:46.133 --> 00:03:49.376
larger the lip ornament, the greater the priest's power.

43
00:03:50.037 --> 00:03:56.042
Humans have pierced many parts of the body for jewelry: the ears, of course, and the nose (through

44
00:03:56.082 --> 00:04:00.045
either the septum or the nostrils) can be ornamented with studs or rings.

45
00:04:01.146 --> 00:04:07.251
In countless cultures, the size and value of the jewelry—whether of bone or precious metal, plain or adorned

46
00:04:07.271 --> 00:04:10.562
with gems—displayed the wealth and status of the wearer.

47
00:04:11.263 --> 00:04:17.768
In many cases, the amount of pain endured during piercing or scarification also conveyed the wearer's status.

48
00:04:18.268 --> 00:04:24.413
Tattooing is achieved by different techniques around the world, but all of them require breaking the skin with

49
00:04:24.473 --> 00:04:25.674
a thin, sharp object.

50
00:04:26.355 --> 00:04:31.819
In Myanmar, a brass tool that resembles a pen is used to slit the skin; then pigment is

51
00:04:31.879 --> 00:04:32.740
rubbed over the wound.

52
00:04:33.404 --> 00:04:40.046
Eskimos, as well as other Arctic and Subarctic tribes, attach a thread dipped in pigment to a needle,

53
00:04:40.746 --> 00:04:43.607
which then inserts the dye as the needle breaks the skin.

54
00:04:44.527 --> 00:04:50.709
The peoples of Polynesia and Micronesia use a small, rakelike tool to prick pigment into the skin.

55
00:04:51.809 --> 00:04:55.451
In modern, sterile tattoo parlors, pain is still part of the process.

56
00:04:56.151 --> 00:05:00.132
Today's sophisticated tattoo guns still pierce the skin to inject the ink.

57
00:05:00.912 --> 00:05:07.176
A precise group of needles attached to a bar is driven into the skin 80 to 150 times

58
00:05:07.256 --> 00:05:10.679
per second, injecting a small amount of ink each time.

59
00:05:11.299 --> 00:05:16.623
Even today, people are most impressed by the largest, and therefore most painful, tattoos.

60
00:05:17.223 --> 00:05:23.547
These traditions may seem harsh, but even more extreme forms of body art, collectively known as "body shaping,"

61
00:05:23.707 --> 00:05:24.588
have been practiced.

62
00:05:25.228 --> 00:05:32.117
For almost 1,500 years, the feet of young girls in China were tightly bound so that their feet

63
00:05:32.177 --> 00:05:34.097
would stay tiny as the girls grew.

64
00:05:34.678 --> 00:05:41.321
Foot binding resulted in broken and deformed feet that made walking difficult and painful, but the small "lotus

65
00:05:41.361 --> 00:05:45.602
feet," as they were called, were considered beautiful and were highly sought after.

66
00:05:46.483 --> 00:05:50.004
The Padaung women of Myanmar extend their necks with brass rings.

67
00:05:50.685 --> 00:05:55.327
The large, shiny rings are first placed around a girl's neck at the age of four or five,

68
00:05:55.867 --> 00:05:57.848
and more rings are added as she ages.

69
00:05:58.568 --> 00:06:03.751
The rings put pressure on the collarbone and shoulders, and make the neck appear an unusual length.

70
00:06:04.431 --> 00:06:09.614
If the rings are removed, the woman's weakened neck may not be able to support her head, forcing

71
00:06:09.654 --> 00:06:10.955
her to remain lying down.

72
00:06:11.675 --> 00:06:16.258
Nonetheless, the Padaung consider long necks an important element of female beauty.

73
00:06:16.938 --> 00:06:20.380
Body shaping is not restricted to ancient or isolated peoples.

74
00:06:21.121 --> 00:06:27.044
The corsets worn by European women in the nineteenth century changed the shape of their ribs, sometimes breaking

75
00:06:27.064 --> 00:06:33.520
them. Made of cloth and strips of whalebone, and laced exceedingly tight, corsets forced women's bodies into a

76
00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:35.081
"perfect" hourglass figure.

77
00:06:36.001 --> 00:06:40.004
The many forms of body art practiced through time have seen a modern comeback.

78
00:06:40.624 --> 00:06:46.908
The availability of surgical steel and clean, sterile methods, which reduce the risk of infection, have made piercings

79
00:06:47.028 --> 00:06:47.668
more popular.

80
00:06:48.469 --> 00:06:53.412
Today, a piercing in the nose, navel, lip, or eyebrow is no more surprising than an earring.

81
00:06:54.048 --> 00:07:00.612
Tattooing was once acceptable only for men in certain segments of society: sailors, soldiers, convicts, and gang members.

82
00:07:01.572 --> 00:07:08.777
Nowadays, society's attitude to tattoos has largely changed, and tattoos are a popular and acceptable method of personal

83
00:07:08.857 --> 00:07:11.698
or artistic expression, for both men and women.

84
00:07:12.439 --> 00:07:18.142
Among the growing body-art subculture, there are women who once again wear corsets with the aim of changing

85
00:07:18.182 --> 00:07:21.364
their body shape, and men who undergo scarification.

86
00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:27.443
Whether it be a teenage girl receiving a henna tattoo for the prom or a body artist displaying

87
00:07:27.503 --> 00:07:33.005
rows of rings in his lips and nose, body art is both a very ancient and very modern

88
00:07:33.045 --> 00:07:33.485
tradition.
